(* ==========================================================================
   catalogue.bnf — WHAT NAMES EXIST.

   `grammar.bnf` says the SHAPE of a program; this says the NAMES one may
   write. Ruling 14 of `SURFACE-PLAN.md`: «dos ficheros de datos y un
   compilador». This is the second file.

   Reference: rust-analyzer's `rust.ungram` — a data file beside the compiler,
   with a test that fails when the Rust drifts from it. Not invented here.

   # What is data and what is not

   A row has two halves and only one of them can live in a file:

     - DATA      the name written after `io.`, the extensions it accepts, and
                 which capabilities it declares. All of it is here.
     - BEHAVIOUR the function that turns bytes into rows or into types. A file
                 cannot carry code, so the implementing crate supplies it and
                 the loader matches it BY NAME.

   That split is rustc's `Providers`: the data says what exists, the code
   registers what it can do. It also buys a diagnostic the tree cannot make
   today — a host that cannot link SHACL currently just lacks the row, so
   `io.shacl("x.ttl")` reports «not an installed row», which is what a typo
   reports too. With the halves separate, "declared but not linked here" and
   "no such name" are different answers.

   # Status

   The rows below ARE the statics. `cargo xtask catalogue` generates
   `crates/fossil-base/src/providers/generated.rs` and
   `crates/fossil-descriptors-output/src/generated.rs` from this file, and
   `cargo xtask catalogue --check` fails when the checked-in output is stale.
   A parity test used to catch the hand-written Rust drifting from these rows;
   there is nothing left to drift.

   Which of the two files a row lands in is DERIVED, not configured, and it is
   the DATA/BEHAVIOUR split above: a row with a `decodes` needs a crate that can
   link a shape-language parser, so it is emitted into `fossil-descriptors-output`;
   every other row into `fossil-base`, which the compiler always has. That is
   also what makes `DATA` — the default of `System::providers` — exactly the
   `fossil-base` half.

   Generation closed one thing the parity test named as beyond it: `decodes
   decode_shex` is now a Rust PATH in the generated file, so the compiler
   resolves it. A row that names a decoder nobody wrote is a build failure, not
   a declared capability with no behaviour behind it.
   ========================================================================== *)

(* ---------------------------------------------------------------- io, data *)
(* Rows that read ROWS. `reads` names how the bytes become a relation:
   `native <fn>` is a DuckDB table function, `materialised` means something
   outside the reader produces the relation and the core only scans it.

   The `native <fn>` token is the ONLY spelling of which reader a row uses.
   `NativeReader`'s variant name is derived from it — strip `read_`, upper-camel
   the rest — and `NativeReader::table_function` gives the token back, so nothing
   downstream re-states it. There were three hand-written derivations of this one
   datum before generation: the enum, a `native_fn` inside the parity test that
   mapped the variant back to the string written here, and a
   `PlanOp::Source(SourceFormatTag)` in `fossil-hir` that nothing ever read.

   `rdf`'s extension list is kept in lockstep with the RDF provider's own. *)

row csv     = extensions "csv"                    ; reads native read_csv_auto  .
row json    = extensions "json"                   ; reads native read_json_auto .
row parquet = extensions "parquet"                ; reads native read_parquet   .
row rdf     = extensions "ttl" "nt" "n3" "rdf"    ; reads materialised          .

(* --------------------------------------------------------------- io, types *)
(* Rows that read TYPES. `decodes` names the function the implementing crate
   registers; there is no `native` here, because a shape language is decoded in
   Rust and never by the database.

   `rdf` and `shacl` both accept `.ttl` and that is not a conflict: the name
   decides. `io.rdf("g.ttl")` reads rows out of a graph, `io.shacl("s.ttl")`
   reads types out of a shapes graph — two capabilities over one syntax, told
   apart by the name, which is the whole of ruling 13. Under the extension
   dispatch this replaced, that pair was unexpressible.

   `shex` does NOT claim `.ttl`, and that is the same rule read the other way:
   `io.shex("catalogue.ttl")` is an error naming the constructor and the
   extension, where before the extension alone chose the row and the constructor
   written in the program was decorative. *)

row shex    = extensions "shex" "shexj" "shexc"   ; decodes decode_shex         .
row shacl   = extensions "ttl" "shacl"            ; decodes decode_shacl        .

(* ------------------------------------------------------------- NOT IN HERE *)
(* The WRITE half of ruling 13's table is deliberately absent, not stubbed:
   `grammar.bnf` says «`io` names only the INPUT», and where a program writes
   to has no syntax at all. A row for it here would be a name no program can
   write.

   The stdlib catalogue — `str.slug`, `parse.date`, the receivers of ADR-0059 —
   is NOT here yet. It is the larger half of ruling 14 (`stdlib.rs` is 1209
   lines of Rust dispatching by string) and it wants the same treatment; this
   file is the shape it will take. *)
